    <article class="prose section">
      <h2>How the disciplines connect</h2><p>Geology provides a way to read the physical world. Geospatial analysis makes spatial relationships explicit. Data engineering gives observations structure, quality and a path into usable systems. Analytical models and software turn that foundation into tools for decisions.</p>
      <h2>Experience and approach</h2><p>My professional work spans environmental, agroclimatic, technological and financial contexts. These are connected by recurring challenges: understanding space and time, designing data architecture, testing analytical assumptions and explaining what a result means.</p><p>I work from diagnosis through implementation: framing questions, structuring data, building APIs and spatial analyses, and making methods understandable. Teaching and documentation are part of this practice because knowledge becomes useful when others can evaluate and apply it.</p>
      <h2>What you can inspect here</h2><p>The <a href="/projects/">selected projects</a> are personal, public experiments in computer vision, media workflows, audio automation and browser privacy. They show implementation choices and limitations; they are not presented as client deliveries or production services.</p><p>Client and corporate work is described only when ownership and disclosure rights allow it.</p>
      <h2>Professional contact and CV</h2><p><a href="/contact/">GitHub is the available contact channel</a>. A downloadable CV, employment timeline and enrollment dates are not published here yet.</p>
    </article>
